what is regression under supervised learning in machine learning?

Regression in Supervised Machine Learning
Regression is a type of supervised learning where the model predicts a continuous numerical value as output, based on input features.

Key Idea
The model learns the relationship between input variables (features) and a continuous output variable (label) from labeled training data, then predicts values for new unseen data.

Simple Example
Predicting house price based on size:

House Size (sq ft)      	Price (₹ lakhs)
1000	                        40
1500	                        60
2000	                        80
The model learns the pattern → predicts price for any new size input.

Common Regression Algorithms
Algorithm	                           Use Case
Linear Regression	             Simple straight-line relationship
Polynomial Regression	         Curved relationships
Ridge / Lasso Regression	     When overfitting is a concern
Decision Tree Regression	     Non-linear data
Random Forest Regression	     Complex, high-accuracy tasks
SVR (Support Vector Regression)	 High-dimensional data

Real-World Applications

🏠 House price prediction
📈 Stock price forecasting
🌡️ Weather temperature prediction
🚗 Car price estimation
💰 Salary prediction based on experience
🏥 Medical cost estimation
Regression vs Classification

          Regression	                                        Classification
Output	   Continuous number (e.g., 85.5)	          Discrete category (e.g., Yes/No)
Example	   Predict salary	                          Predict if email is spam
Metrics	   MSE, RMSE, R²	                          Accuracy, Precision, Recall

How it Works (Linear Regression)
The model fits a best-fit line:

y = mx + b
y = predicted output
x = input feature
m = slope (weight)
b = intercept (bias)
The goal is to minimize the error (difference between predicted and actual values) using a method called Gradient Descent.

In short: If your output is a number, use Regression. If it's a category, use Classification.

